/* generated from the course pages */
window.ENT142_INDEX=[{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s0","n":"Start here","t":"How do we turn a social problem into a venture people use?","x":"ENT 142 continues ENT 141. This semester your team takes one real social problem, linked to a UN Sustainable Development Goal, and turns it into a social venture with evidence. Syllabus topics: Overview of the Course · Course Material · Course Assessment · Revisiting the Design Thinking Process · Recap of the DTP · Step 4: Prototyping · Step 5: Testing. CLO 1: Explain the process of innovation, entrepreneurship and sustainability. CLO 3: Analyze the impact of innovation, entrepreneurship and sustainability on society and the economy."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s1","n":"Course map","t":"Course material and assessment","x":"Material: textbook Byers, Dorf & Nelson (2019), Technology ventures; reference books by Liedtka et al. (2017), Ray & Shaw (2023) and Bridge et al. (2020); HBR articles; weekly pages on this site; announcements on Moodle. Assessment: the bar on the right is drawn to scale. Half of your grade is Project 1, built step by step in the Project 1 Lab from Week 5. IN PLAIN WORDS Project 1 report and presentation: Tuesday 27 October, same day, submitted on Moodle. Other dates follow the syllabus."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s2","n":"Hook","t":"Hook · Where does design thinking start?","x":"Vote before we recap."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s3","n":"Design thinking recap","t":"Recap of the design thinking process","x":"IN PLAIN WORDS Five steps, used in loops, not a straight line: Empathize (understand people) → Define (state the real problem) → Ideate (many ideas) → Prototype (make it tangible) → Test (learn from users). SIMPLE EXAMPLE Bank of America and the design firm IDEO noticed through research that customers rounded up their transactions for speed and convenience. The result, Keep the Change (October 2005), rounds each debit-card purchase up to the next dollar and moves the difference into savings. Bloomberg reported 2.5 million customers signed up, with more than 700,000 new checking accounts and 1 "},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s4","n":"Define your problem","t":"Activity · Define your problem","x":"In your team, write one point-of-view statement and turn it into a “How might we…” question. SIMPLE EXAMPLE POV: Students who eat on campus in the evening need affordable meals because they skip dinner when food is expensive. HMW: How might we make good evening meals affordable for students without adding waste?"},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s5","n":"Step 4 · Prototyping","t":"Step 4 · Prototyping (preview)","x":"IN PLAIN WORDS A prototype is the cheapest thing that lets a user experience your idea: a sketch, paper screens, a storyboard, a cardboard model or a role-play. You build it to learn, not to impress. SIMPLE EXAMPLE Before the PalmPilot existed, Jeff Hawkins carried a wooden model in his pocket and used a chopstick as a stylus to test how the device would fit into his day. Week 5 covers prototyping in depth, with a Prototype Helper that recommends a technique for your idea."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s6","n":"Step 5 · Testing","t":"Step 5 · Testing","x":"How to test: give the user a task, stay silent, watch what they do, then ask open questions. Do not explain or defend your idea. IN PLAIN WORDS Capture feedback in four boxes: I like · I wish · Questions · What if. This feedback grid is widely used in design thinking. SIMPLE EXAMPLE A tester taps the wrong button twice on your paper screen. That is not a user mistake; it is a design finding. Write it under I wish."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s7","n":"Test exchange","t":"Activity · Test exchange","x":"Use your ENT 141 prototype, or sketch one in 5 minutes. Swap with the team on your left: each team tests the other's prototype for 8 minutes, then switches. Fill in the feedback grid with what your testers said and did."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s8","n":"Key takeaways","t":"Key takeaways · Week 1","x":"REMEMBER FOR YOUR PROJECT Project 1 (report 40% + presentation 10%) is half the grade. Design thinking: empathize → define → ideate → prototype → test, in loops. Define the problem as [user] needs [need] because [insight]. Prototype cheaply; test by watching, not explaining. Capture feedback: I like · I wish · Questions · What if."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#s9","n":"Exit check","t":"Exit check","x":"Five questions. Before Week 2: read Byers et al (2019). Ch. 1. Each member brings 3 ideas for a social venture, each linked to a problem and an SDG."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s0","n":"Start here","t":"Which idea deserves our next 12 weeks?","x":"Today each team chooses one idea to develop all semester, and forms its final team. Syllabus topics: Idea Selection and Evaluation · How to Identify Effective Ideas · Discussion of Ideas · Group Formation. CLO 1 and CLO 3."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s1","n":"Hook","t":"Hook · Is the most creative idea the best one?","x":"Vote."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s2","n":"Effective ideas","t":"How to identify effective ideas","x":"IN PLAIN WORDS Test every idea on four lenses. Desirability: do real people need it? Feasibility: can your team build it with your skills and time? Viability: can it pay for itself? Impact: does it move your SDG forward? SIMPLE EXAMPLE “An app for everyone to be healthier” fails desirability (too vague) and feasibility (too big). “A weekly fruit-box pickup for students in one residence hall” is specific, buildable and testable."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s3","n":"Idea or opportunity?","t":"Idea or opportunity?","x":"An idea is a solution you thought of. An opportunity is an idea with a real problem, a reachable customer and a reason now. SIMPLE EXAMPLE Idea: “a recycling app.” Opportunity: “students in shared housing in Dubai do not know where to drop e-waste; a map of drop-off points near campuses saves them a trip.”"},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s4","n":"Pitch round","t":"Activity · Discussion of ideas: pitch round","x":"Each member pitches their best idea in 60 seconds: problem → who → solution → why now. Listeners give one line each: I like · I wish · What if. Then each person places 2 dots (votes) on the ideas they would work on all semester."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s5","n":"Score your ideas","t":"Activity · Score your top ideas","x":"Take the 3–5 ideas with the most dots. Score each on the four lenses, adjust the weights if your team agrees, and read the ranking. The matrix supports the decision; it does not make it. If the top two are close, choose the one you can test fastest."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s6","n":"Form your team","t":"Activity · Group formation","x":"Teams of 4–5, built around the chosen ideas. Give every member a lead role; everyone still contributes to every section. Register your team (CUD account) → ROLE LEADS IN THE PROJECT 1 LAB Coordinator Plan, deadlines, final assembly (Step 11) Data lead Verified data and market (Steps 2, 5) Customer lead Customers and competition (Steps 4, 6) Design lead Prototype (Step 8) Business lead Business model (Step 7)"},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s7","n":"Key takeaways","t":"Key takeaways · Week 2","x":"REMEMBER FOR YOUR PROJECT An effective idea passes four lenses: desirability, feasibility, viability, impact. An opportunity = real problem + reachable customer + reason now. Pitch in 60 seconds: problem → who → solution → why now. Use a weighted matrix to support, not replace, the team decision. Teams of 4–5 with a charter and lead roles."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#s8","n":"Exit check","t":"Exit check","x":"Five questions. Before Week 3: read Byers et al (2019). Ch. 4 and the HBR article: Blank, S. (2013). Why the lean start-up changes everything. Harvard Business Review."},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#s0","n":"Start here","t":"How do we learn what customers want before time runs out?","x":"Your team has one idea. This week you plan the cheapest experiment that tells you whether people want it. Syllabus topics: Lean Startups · Introduction to Lean Startups · Minimum Viable Product (MVP). CLO 1 and CLO 3."},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#s1","n":"Hook","t":"Hook · What kills most new ventures?","x":"Vote."},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#s2","n":"Lean startup","t":"Introduction to lean startups","x":"IN PLAIN WORDS A lean startup treats every plan as a set of guesses (hypotheses) and tests them quickly with real customers. Steve Blank (2013) summarises it as experimentation over elaborate planning, customer feedback over intuition, and iterative design over big up-front design. The engine is one loop, from Eric Ries (2011): Build → Measure → Learn. Each loop should be short and cheap. SIMPLE EXAMPLE Guess: “students will pay 15 AED for a surplus meal.” Build: a landing page with the offer. Measure: 31 sign-ups in a week. Learn: interest is real but below the target of 50; test a lower pric"},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#s3","n":"MVP","t":"Minimum Viable Product (MVP)","x":"IN PLAIN WORDS An MVP is the version of a new product that lets a team collect the maximum amount of validated learning about customers with the least effort (Ries, 2011). It is an experiment, not a small version of the final product. Five common MVP types. Click each for a real case."},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#s4","n":"MVP or not?","t":"Activity · MVP or not?","x":"Sort each plan. Then reveal and discuss."},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#s5","n":"Pivot or persevere","t":"Pivot, iterate or persevere?","x":"Before you run an MVP, write down a target number. After the test, compare the result with the target and decide. SIMPLE EXAMPLE Target 50 sign-ups, result 31: iterate (change one thing). Result 8: pivot (change the customer, problem or solution). Result 65: persevere."},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#s6","n":"Design your MVP","t":"Activity · Design your MVP experiment","x":"Fill in the experiment card for your team's idea. Run the MVP before Week 5 if you can: the result becomes evidence for Steps 4 and 8 of the Project 1 Lab. SIMPLE EXAMPLE We believe university students near campus will reserve a discounted evening meal. MVP: landing page shared in 3 class groups for 7 days. We will know we are right when 50 students sign up."},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#s7","n":"Key takeaways","t":"Key takeaways · Week 3","x":"REMEMBER FOR YOUR PROJECT Lean startup: test guesses with customers before building. Build → Measure → Learn, in short loops. MVP = maximum validated learning with the least effort. Five MVP types: landing page, explainer video, concierge, Wizard of Oz, single feature. Set a target number before the test; then persevere, iterate or pivot."},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#s8","n":"Exit check","t":"Exit check","x":"Five questions. Before Week 4: read the UN SDGs (sdgs.un.org/goals), Qian et al., (2024) and Bridge et al 2020 Ch1."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#s0","n":"Start here","t":"Can a venture make money and make things better?","x":"This week you place your venture in the social economy, measure it on the triple bottom line, and link it to a specific SDG target. Syllabus topics: Sustainable Innovation and Entrepreneurship · Introduction to the Third Sector and Social Economy · Recap of Triple Bottom-line Framework · Recap of SDGs and Linking the SDGs to the Product. CLO 1 and CLO 3."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#s1","n":"Hook","t":"Hook · Social or profitable?","x":"Vote."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#s2","n":"Third sector","t":"Introduction to the third sector and social economy","x":"IN PLAIN WORDS Three sectors: the public sector (government), the private sector (businesses seeking profit) and the third sector (organisations driven by a social mission: charities, NGOs, foundations, cooperatives, social enterprises). The social economy is the economic activity of these mission-driven organisations. SIMPLE EXAMPLE Charity model: the UAE Food Bank (launched 2017) collects surplus food from hotels, restaurants and grocery chains and distributes it to people in need. Social business model: Grameen Bank in Bangladesh gives small loans without collateral, mostly to women; Muhamm"},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#s3","n":"Which sector?","t":"Activity · Which sector?","x":"Sort each organisation (descriptions are generic). Then decide where your venture sits."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#s4","n":"Triple bottom line","t":"Recap of the triple bottom line","x":"IN PLAIN WORDS John Elkington introduced the triple bottom line in 1994: judge a business on People (social), Planet (environmental) and Profit (economic), not on profit alone. SIMPLE EXAMPLE For a surplus-meal app: People: affordable meals sold to students per month. Planet: kilograms of food saved from landfill. Profit: monthly surplus after costs. Each one is a number you can track."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#s5","n":"SDGs recap","t":"Recap of the SDGs","x":"The 17 Sustainable Development Goals were adopted by all UN member states in 2015 as part of the 2030 Agenda. Each goal has targets (169 in total), and each target has indicators. IN PLAIN WORDS Linking to “SDG 12” is too vague. Link to a target: e.g. 12.3: “By 2030, halve per capita global food waste at the retail and consumer levels and reduce food losses along production and supply chains, including post-harvest losses.”"},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#s6","n":"Link to a target","t":"Activity · Link your product to an SDG target","x":"Build the chain from what your product does to the SDG target it moves. Each link must be something you could measure. SIMPLE EXAMPLE Product: app listing surplus café meals → Output: 2,000 meals sold a month → Outcome: less food thrown away near campus → Measure: kg of food saved → Target: SDG 12.3."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#s7","n":"Key takeaways","t":"Key takeaways · Week 4","x":"REMEMBER FOR YOUR PROJECT Three sectors: public, private, third; the social economy is mission-driven activity. Social enterprises earn revenue and put the mission first. Triple bottom line (Elkington, 1994): People, Planet, Profit, each with a measurable indicator. 17 SDGs, 169 targets: link your product to a target, not just a goal. Impact chain: product → output → outcome → measure → target. These answers go into Steps 3 (Concept) and 7 (Business model) of the Project 1 Lab."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#s8","n":"Exit check","t":"Exit check","x":"Five questions. Before Week 5: read Byers et al (2019). Ch. 8, Pradeep & Rajib (2022). Bring your idea, your SDG target and your MVP result."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s0","n":"Start here","t":"How do we turn an idea into something people can experience?","x":"Your team already has a social problem, an idea and an SDG. Today we make the idea visible, testable and easier to explain, and we start Project 1. Syllabus topics: Prototyping · Prototype Generation Techniques · Initial Prototype Development. This builds CLO 1: Explain the process of innovation, entrepreneurship and sustainability."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s1","n":"What first?","t":"Hook · What should the team do first?","x":"A team wants an app that lets the CUD cafeteria sell surplus meals cheaply at the end of the day. They have 5 days. Vote: what should they do first?"},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s2","n":"What is a prototype?","t":"A prototype makes an idea visible","x":"IN PLAIN WORDS A prototype is a simple representation of your solution that lets you see it, explain it and test it before you fully develop it. See: what does it look like? Experience: how does the user interact? Test: what can we learn? Prototypes range from low fidelity (a sketch, in minutes) to high fidelity (a working demo, in days). Start low. Move up only when the cheap version has taught you all it can. SIMPLE EXAMPLE Jeff Hawkins tested the PalmPilot design by carrying a wooden model in his pocket, using a chopstick as a stylus. He took pretend notes in meetings and counted the steps "},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s3","n":"Six techniques","t":"Prototype generation techniques","x":"Six techniques, from the slide: Sketch (show the idea), Storyboard (show the user journey), Paper (simulate interaction), Digital (show screens or interface), Physical (show the product), Role-play (simulate the service). Click each technique on the right for an example. Three are real company stories; the others are student-scale examples."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s4","n":"Prototype Helper","t":"Choose your prototype · Prototype Helper","x":"There is no single right prototype. Look at your project and its SDG, then choose the fastest, cheapest, easiest one that tests your biggest assumption and collects feedback before you invest. Try it: click an example idea to load it into the box, change it to your own idea, and press Send. Answer three questions and copy the plan into the Build v1 worksheet. SIMPLE EXAMPLE Idea: a WhatsApp service that matches retired professionals in Dubai with students for career mentoring. Biggest assumption: retirees will reply within a day. Prototype: run it by hand for one week with 3 mentors and 5 stud"},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s5","n":"Real prototypes","t":"How real teams prototype and show it","x":"Watch one video. Notice how rough the first prototypes are, and how fast they test them. SIMPLE EXAMPLE Incubators showcase startups with the prototype in people's hands. in5, the TECOM Group incubator in Dubai, brought seven of its startups to GITEX 2022 to show their products to a wide audience. Show it like a startup: demo first → the problem it solves → what testers did → what you will test next."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s6","n":"3D with Meshy AI","t":"A quick 3D prototype with Meshy AI","x":"For a physical product, an AI tool can turn a sketch or a sentence into a 3D model in minutes. Press ▶ Play to see the steps. Meshy free plan: 100 credits a month, up to 10 models, licence CC BY 4.0 (credit Meshy). IN PLAIN WORDS A 3D model shows the look. It does not prove people want it: put it in front of users and test."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s7","n":"Case: Embrace","t":"Case · Embrace infant warmer","x":"In 2007, Stanford students Jane Chen, Rahul Panicker and Linus Liang worked on hypothermia in low-birth-weight babies in the course Design for Extreme Affordability. IN PLAIN WORDS Their early prototype looked like a small sleeping bag for babies, with a reusable pouch of phase-change material that, once heated, stays at body temperature for several hours. No constant electricity needed. SIMPLE EXAMPLE Hospital incubators can cost up to $20,000; Embrace was designed to cost about $200. Observing the needs of rural families in Nepal led the team to add a second, at-home model for mothers in rur"},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s8","n":"Match the technique","t":"Activity 1 · Match the technique","x":"In your team, pick the best first prototype for each idea. Then reveal the suggestions and discuss any you disagree with."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s9","n":"Build v1","t":"Activity 2 · Build your initial prototype","x":"Answer the four questions on the right as a team, then build the simplest version today. Use what you have: paper, Canva, slides, cardboard, a mock-up or role-play. Last 10 minutes: test it with another team. They play your user. Write down what they did, what confused them, and one thing to change. REMEMBER FOR YOUR PROJECT Take photos of every version and keep your notes. The prototype is not a separate exercise: it becomes evidence in Project 1."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s10","n":"Prototype → Project 1","t":"Project 1 starts here","x":"You already have the problem, idea and SDG. Now you develop the venture into a complete Social Enterprise Project. Week 5: prototype + project briefing Week 6: market + competition Then: business model, pitching and the Project 1 report (see the course map for the syllabus weeks). REMEMBER FOR YOUR PROJECT Next: Project 1 Lab, Steps 1–3."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s11","n":"Key takeaways","t":"Key takeaways · Week 5","x":"REMEMBER FOR YOUR PROJECT If you remember only this… Prototype = simple representation to see, explain and test an idea. Goal = learning, not perfection. Six techniques: sketch, storyboard, paper, digital, physical, role-play. No single right type: choose the fastest, cheapest, easiest prototype that tests your biggest assumption. Start low fidelity; climb only when needed. Evidence: photos, test notes and changes go into Project 1. Use the questions below to check your team is ready for next week."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#s12","n":"Exit check","t":"Exit check","x":"Six quick questions. Before Week 6: read Byers et al (2019). Ch. 4,7. Bring your prototype photos and test notes. Week 6 covers Industry Barriers, Identifying Competition, Positioning and Positioning Map, Porter's 5 Forces and the Competition Matrix."},{"pt":"Project 1 Lab","l":"project-1.html#s0","n":"Overview","t":"Project 1 Lab · 11 steps to your report","x":"Task: Register your team, then work through the steps in order. Each step ends with a draft section and a check. Output: A complete, evidence-based report draft. Register your team (CUD account) →"},{"pt":"Project 1 Lab","l":"project-1.html#s1","n":"1 · Venture card","t":"Step 1 · Venture card","x":"Task: Describe your venture in five fields. Output: Your one-line value proposition. Exploring first? Load the example: a fictional venture with real data."},{"pt":"Project 1 Lab","l":"project-1.html#s2","n":"2 · Verified data","t":"Step 2 · Collect verified data","x":"Task: find at least 5 numbers about your problem, customers and market on trusted sites. Add each as a data card. Where: UAE: fcsc.gov.ae · bayanat.ae · Dubai Data and Statistics Establishment · scad.gov.ae · ministries (*.gov.ae). World: data.worldbank.org · unstats.un.org/sdgs/dataportal · unep.org · who.int/data · ourworldindata.org. Research: Google Scholar, CUD library. Search: food waste site:gov.ae · \"statistical yearbook\" Dubai filetype:pdf. News and Wikipedia only to find the original source. Chatbots are not sources. Verify: who published it · year of the data · how it was measured ·"},{"pt":"Project 1 Lab","l":"project-1.html#s3","n":"3 · Concept","t":"Step 3 · Concept and Vision","x":"Task: Answer the questions. Name two similar ventures and link one data card. Output: Concept and Vision draft + problem → solution → impact figure."},{"pt":"Project 1 Lab","l":"project-1.html#s4","n":"4 · Customers","t":"Step 4 · Customers and Customer Development","x":"Task: Describe your customer, link one card for how many people are affected and one for how serious the problem is, then show assumption → evidence → change. Output: Customer draft + customer profile figure."},{"pt":"Project 1 Lab","l":"project-1.html#s5","n":"5 · Market","t":"Step 5 · Market Analysis","x":"Task: Pick a verified total (TAM), set SAM % and SOM %, and the yearly spend per customer. Label assumptions. Output: Market draft + TAM/SAM/SOM figure with year-1 value."},{"pt":"Project 1 Lab","l":"project-1.html#s6","n":"6 · Competition","t":"Step 6 · Competition and Positioning","x":"Task: List 3 competitors (direct, indirect, substitute). Score everyone 1–5 on two things customers care about. Output: Competition draft + positioning map."},{"pt":"Project 1 Lab","l":"project-1.html#s7","n":"7 · Business model","t":"Step 7 · Sustainable Business Model","x":"Task: Enter who pays, prices, monthly volumes and costs, one people and one planet indicator, and your biggest risk. Output: Business model draft + revenue vs cost and triple-bottom-line figure."},{"pt":"Project 1 Lab","l":"project-1.html#s8","n":"8 · Prototype","t":"Step 8 · Prototype Development","x":"Task: Report your Week 5 prototype: assumption, test, result, version 2. Unsure of the technique? Use the Prototype Helper. Output: Prototype draft + v1 → test → v2 figure."},{"pt":"Project 1 Lab","l":"project-1.html#s9","n":"9 · Learning","t":"Step 9 · Learning and Adaptation","x":"Task: Each member completes their own three lines. Then answer as a team: is this a true opportunity? Output: One paragraph per member + team verdict."},{"pt":"Project 1 Lab","l":"project-1.html#s10","n":"10 · Intro + summary","t":"Step 10 · Introduction and Executive Summary","x":"Task: Describe your creative process and next step. The rest is built from Steps 1–9. Output: Introduction (max 250 words) + executive summary (max 1 page)."},{"pt":"Project 1 Lab","l":"project-1.html#s11","n":"11 · Assemble","t":"Step 11 · Assemble, check and submit","x":"Task: Copy the full draft into Word in the brief's order, expand it to about 4000 words, add cover page and contents, and tick every check. Output: Your submitted report."},{"pt":"Project 1 Lab","l":"project-1.html#s12","n":"Scorecard","t":"Validation scorecard","x":"Task: Read your score. Strengthen any red or amber line before you submit. Output: Evidence for “Is this a true opportunity?” (Step 9)."},{"pt":"Project 1 Lab","l":"project-1.html#s13","n":"Marking","t":"How it is marked","x":"Rubrics as written in the syllabus and the Week 5 briefing, plus the presentation brief."}];
